BT164.16 710.88  TD0 0 0 rg /F0 13.92  Tf0.0331  Tc -0.0331  Tw (Behavioral Types for Component-Based Design) Tj57.96 -25.68  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0034  Tc 0.0034  Tw (Edward A. Lee and Yuhong Xiong) Tj-64.32 -14.04  TD -0.0039  Tc 0.0039  Tw (Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Sciences) Tj65.88 -14.04  TD 0.0039  Tc -0.0039  Tw (University of California, Berkeley) Tj17.88 -13.92  TD 0  Tc -0  Tw (Berkeley, CA 94720, USA) Tj-17.4 -14.04  TD -0.002  Tc 0.002  Tw ({eal, yuhong}@eecs.berkeley.edu) Tj-124.2 -36.6  TD /F0 9.96  Tf0.0324  Tc 0  Tw (Abstract) Tj37.08 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.021  Tc -0.087  Tw (. We present a framework to extend the concept of type systems in programming languages to) Tj-37.08 -12  TD 0.0176  Tc 1.1884  Tw (capture the dynamic interaction in component-based design, including the communication protocols) Tj0 -12  TD 0.0154  Tc 0.606  Tw (between components. Our system is based on a light-weight formalism - interface automata. We first) TjT* 0.0135  Tc 0.0315  Tw (propose some extensions to the theory of interface automata to make them more powerful, then use the) TjT* 0.0104  Tc -0.0604  Tw (extended theory to establish a behavioral-level type system. In our system, the interaction types and the) TjT* 0.0177  Tc 1.9523  Tw (dynamic behavior of components are defined using interface automata, and type checking, which) TjT* 0.0181  Tc -0.0309  Tw (checks the compatibility of a component with a certain interaction type, is conducted through automata) TjT* 0.0159  Tc 0.4221  Tw (composition. Our type system is polymorphic in that a component may be compatible with more than) TjT* 0.0188  Tc 0.0192  Tw (one interaction type. We show that a subtyping relation exists among various interaction types and that) TjT* 0.0116  Tc 0.322  Tw (this relation can be described using a partial order. This behavioral type order can be used to facilitate) TjT* 0.0127  Tc 0.1373  Tw (the design of polymorphic components and simplify type checking. In addition to static type checking,) TjT* 0.019  Tc 0.6785  Tw (we also propose to extend the use of interface automata to the on-line reflection of component states) TjT* 0.0167  Tc 0.3548  Tw (and to run-time type checking. We illustrate our framework using a component-based design environ-) TjT* 0.0149  Tc -0.0126  Tw (ment, Ptolemy II, and discuss the trade-offs in the design of behavioral type systems.) Tj0 -24  TD /F0 9.96  Tf0.0204  Tc 0  Tw (Keywords) Tj43.2 0  TD /F1 9.96  Tf0.016  Tc -0.181  Tw (: Behavioral type; Component-based design; Interface automata; Polymorphism; Alternating) Tj-43.2 -12  TD 0.0299  Tc 0  Tw (simulation) Tj-28.08 -38.76  TD /F2 13.92  Tf0.0397  Tc -0.0694  Tw (1. Introduction) Tj0 -18.6  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0048  Tc 0.9905  Tw (Type systems are one of the most successful formal methods in software design. Modern poly-) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0045  Tc 1.1953  Tw (morphic type systems, with their early error detection capabilities and the support for software) TjT* -0.0017  Tc 0.0017  Tw (reuse, have led to considerable improvements in development productivity and software quality.) Tj14.4 -13.92  TD -0.0049  Tc 1.4231  Tw (For embedded systems, component-based design is established as an important approach to) Tj-14.4 -14.04  TD -0.0037  Tc 0.0487  Tw (handle complexity. In this area, type systems can be used to greatly improve the quality of design) Tj0 -14.04  TD 0.0009  Tc 1.9671  Tw (environments. Fundamentally, a type system detects mismatches at component interfaces and) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0033  Tc -0.1567  Tw (ensures component compatibility. Interface mismatch can happen at \(at least\) two different levels.) Tj0 -14.04  TD 0.0013  Tc 0.3253  Tw (One is the data type level. For example, if a component expects to receive an integer at its input,) TjT* -0.0031  Tc 0.0031  Tw (but another component sends it a string, then the first component may not be able to function cor-) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0049  Tc 0.8818  Tw (rectly. Many type system techniques in general purpose languages can be applied effectively to) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0044  Tc 0.0604  Tw (ensure compatibility at this level \(see [XiL00] and the references therein\). The other level of mis-) TjT* -0.0047  Tc 0.7547  Tw (match is the dynamic interaction behavior, such as the communication protocol the components) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0  Tc 0.02  Tw (use to exchange data. Since embedded systems often have many concurrent computational activi-) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0054  Tc 1.0527  Tw (ties and mix widely differing operations, components may follow widely different communica-) TjT* 0.0013  Tc 0.7841  Tw (tion protocols. For example, some might use synchronous interaction \(rendezvous\) while others) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0033  Tc 0.0548  Tw (use asynchronous message passing \(see [Lee02] for many more examples\). So far, most type sys-) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0017  Tc 0.2217  Tw (tem research for component-based design, as well as for general purpose languages, concentrates) TjT* -0.0014  Tc 0.0014  Tw (on data types, and leaves the checking of dynamic behavior to other techniques.) Tj14.4 -13.92  TD 0  Tc 0.0222  Tw (In this paper, we extend the concept of type systems to capture the dynamic aspects of compo-) Tj-14.4 -14.04  TD 0.0058  Tc 0.6182  Tw (nent interaction. We call the result) Tj0  Tc 0  Tw ( ) Tj172.2 0  TD /F3 12  Tf0.0096  Tc 0.5904  Tw (behavioral types) Tj80.28 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0036  Tc 0.6636  Tw (. In our approach, different interaction types) Tj-250.92 -46.68  TD /F3 9  Tf-0.0041  Tc -0.0028  Tw (Technical Memorandum UCB/ERL, M02/29, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA, September 27, 2002.) TjETendstreamendobj11 0 obj5718endobj4 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 5 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F0 6 0 R /F1 8 0 R /F2 12 0 R /F3 14 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 10 0 R>>endobj17 0 obj<</Length 18 0 R>>stream
