ned earlier, modern) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0022  Tc 0.1542  Tw (polymorphic type systems have achieved this goal to a large extend. At the behavioral level, type) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0041  Tc 0.0641  Tw (systems should also be polymorphic to support component reuse while ensuring component com-) TjT* -0.0022  Tc 0.0022  Tw (patibility. ) Tj14.4 -13.92  TD -0  Tc -0.2997  Tw (In programming languages, there are several kinds of polymorphism. In [CaW85], Cardelli and) Tj-14.4 -14.04  TD -0.002  Tc 1.322  Tw (Wegner distinguished two broad kinds of polymorphism:) Tj0  Tc -0.12  Tw ( ) Tj286.8 0  TD /F3 12  Tf0.0107  Tc 0  Tw (universal) Tj44.64 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0.024  Tc 1.296  Tw ( and) Tj0  Tc 0  Tw ( ) Tj25.92 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.0096  Tc 1.3296  Tw (ad hoc) Tj33.6 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0129  Tc 1.3329  Tw ( polymorphism.) Tj-390.96 -14.04  TD -0.0018  Tc 1.3698  Tw (Universal polymorphism is further divided into) Tj0  Tc 0  Tw ( ) Tj237.96 0  TD /F3 12  Tf0.0072  Tc (parametric) Tj53.28 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.016  Tc 1.456  Tw ( and) Tj0  Tc 0  Tw ( ) Tj26.04 0  TD /F3 12  Tf0.004  Tc (inclusion) Tj44.04 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0027  Tc 1.3227  Tw ( polymorphism. Para-) Tj-361.32 -13.92  TD -0.0027  Tc 0.0713  Tw (metric polymorphism is obtained when a function works uniformly on a range of types. Inclusion) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0008  Tc 1.3048  Tw (polymorphism appears in object oriented languages when a subclass can be used in place of ) Tj1.272  Tc 0  Tw (a) TjT* -0.0049  Tc 0.9649  Tw (superclass. Ad hoc polymorphism is also further divided into overloading and coercion. In sys-) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.009  Tc 0.5833  Tw (tems with subtyping and coercion, types naturally form a partial order [DaP90]. For example, in) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0029  Tc -0.1725  Tw (object-oriented languages, the partial order is the inheritance hierarchy, and in languages that sup-) TjT* -0.0017  Tc -0.0623  Tw (port type conversion, the relation in the partial order is the conversion relation, such as ) Tj417.84 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.004  Tc 0  Tw (Int) Tj13.32 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc ( ) Tj3 0  TD /F4 12  Tf0.012  Tc (\243) Tj6.6 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc ( ) Tj2.88 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.015  Tc (Dou-) Tj-443.64 -13.92  TD 0.032  Tc (ble) Tj14.64 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0034  Tc 0.7909  Tw (, which means that an integer can be converted to a double. This latter relation is sometimes) Tj-14.64 -14.04  TD -0.0026  Tc -0.1517  Tw (considered as subtyping between primitive data types [Mit84]. In the Ptolemy II data type system,) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.002  Tc 0.978  Tw (the type hierarchy if further constrained to be a lattice, and type constraints are formulated and) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0024  Tc 0.0024  Tw (solved over the lattice [XiL00][Xio02].) Tj14.4 -14.04  TD -0.0068  Tc 1.3697  Tw (We form a polymorphic type system at the behavioral level through an approach similar to) Tj-14.4 -14.04  TD -0.0042  Tc 1.6242  Tw (subtyping. Using the alternating simulation relation of interface automata, we organize all the) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0037  Tc 0.1762  Tw (interaction types in a partial order. Given this hierarchy, if a component is compatible with a cer-) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.003  Tc 0.063  Tw (tain type ) Tj44.76 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.012  Tc 0  Tw (A) Tj7.32 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0  Tc 0.0843  Tw (, it is also compatible with all the subtypes of ) Tj221.04 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.012  Tc 0  Tw (A) Tj7.32 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0.085  Tw (. This property can be used to facilitate) Tj-280.44 -14.04  TD -0.0021  Tc 0.0021  Tw (the design of polymorphic components and simplify type checking.) Tj14.4 -13.92  TD -0  Tc 0.8483  Tw (Even with the power of polymorphism, no type system can capture all the properties of pro-) Tj-14.4 -14.04  TD -0.0021  Tc 0.7682  Tw (grams and allow type checking to be performed efficiently while keeping the language flexible.) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0038  Tc 0.3488  Tw (So the language designer always has to decide what properties to