ured by alternating simulation. We propose to) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0049  Tc 0.3649  Tw (extend the theory of interface automata to address these issue, and report these extensions in this) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.002  Tc 0  Tw (paper.) Tj14.4 -14.04  TD -0.0029  Tc 0.1589  Tw (The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section ) Tj261.72 0  TD -0.0036  Tc 0.1636  Tw (2 gives an overview of interface autom-) Tj-276.12 -13.92  TD -0.0065  Tc 1.6865  Tw (ata. Section) Tj0  Tc 0  Tw ( ) Tj62.16 0  TD -0  Tc 1.62  Tw (3 discusses our extensions. Section 4 describes Ptolemy II, with emphasis on the) Tj-62.16 -14.04  TD 0  Tc 0.3398  Tw (implementation of various communication protocols. Section ) Tj299.28 0  TD -0.0056  Tc 0.4376  Tw (5 presents our behavioral type sys-) Tj-299.28 -14.04  TD -0.0051  Tc -0.1664  Tw (tem, including the type definition, the type hierarchy and some type checking examples. Section 6) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0023  Tc 1.5451  Tw (discusses some issues in the behavioral type systems and related works. The last section con-) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.0024  Tc 0.0024  Tw (cludes the paper and points out our future research directions.) Tj0 -29.28  TD /F2 13.92  Tf0.0436  Tc -0.0434  Tw (2. Overview of Interface Automata) Tj0 -27.36  TD /F2 12.96  Tf0.0171  Tc -0.02  Tw (2.1 An Example) Tj0 -17.4  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0018  Tc 1.5518  Tw (Interface automata [deH01] are a light-weight formalism for the modeling of components and) Tj0 -13.92  TD 0.0021  Tc 0.1455  Tw (their environments. As with other automata models, interface automata consist of states and tran-) Tj0 -14.04  TD 0.0137  Tc 0  Tw (sitions) Tj31.32 4.8  TD /F1 9.6  Tf0  Tc (1) Tj4.8 -4.8  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0025  Tc 0.5841  Tw (, and are usually depicted by bubble-and-arc diagrams. There are three different kinds of) Tj-36.12 -14.04  TD 0  Tc 1.1797  Tw (transitions in interface automata: input, output, and internal transitions. When modeling a soft-) Tj0 -13.92  TD 0  Tc 0.2579  Tw (ware component, input transitions correspond to the invocation of methods on the component, or) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0  Tc -0.2211  Tw (the returning of method calls from other components. Output transitions correspond to the invoca-) TjT* -0.0008  Tc 0.1208  Tw (tion of methods on other components, or the returning of method calls from the component being) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.0045  Tc 0.0045  Tw (modeled. Internal transitions correspond to computations inside the component.) Tj14.4 -14.04  TD -0.0027  Tc 0.1727  Tw (In behavioral-level modeling, one of the most frequently used examples is buffered communi-) Tj-14.4 -14.04  TD -0.0017  Tc 0.6417  Tw (cation. We will use interface automata to model such a scenario. Assume we have two software) Tj0 -13.92  TD -0.007  Tc 0.067  Tw (components, a ) Tj72.36 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.0105  Tc 0  Tw (Producer) Tj45.24 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0.006  Tc 0.074  Tw ( and a ) Tj31.8 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.0015  Tc 0  Tw (Consumer) Tj49.32 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0.0028  Tc 0.0122  Tw (, and that they communicate through a one-place buffer.) Tj-198.72 -14.04  TD -0  Tc 0.6503  Tw (The buffer component has the following methods: put\(\), get\(\), hasRoom\(\), and hasToken\(\). The) Tj0 -14.04  TD -0.003  Tc -0.057  Tw (producer uses hasRoom\(\) to check whether the buffer has room for a token. If this method returns) Tj0 -13.92  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.003  Tc 0  Tw (true) Tj19.32 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.0015  Tc 0.4251  Tw (, it calls the put\(\) method to put a token into the buffer. Similarly, the consumer uses hasTo-) Tj-19.32 -14.04  TD -0.0025  Tc 1.1625  Tw (ken\(\) to check whether the buffer contains a token. If this method returns) Tj0  Tc 0  Tw ( ) Tj368.76 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.033  Tc (true) Tj19.32 0  TD /F1 12  Tf-0.004  Tc 1.114  Tw (, it calls get\(\) to) Tj-388.08 -14.04  TD -0.0023  Tc -0.1844  Tw (extract the token. For the moment, let\222s just model the part of the buffer interface used by the con-) Tj0 -13.92  TD 0.0023  Tc 1.4